\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Fidelity design rule. The cost gap a copperplate misses (the loss burden) against the a-priori loss number $\lambda =\text  {annual loss}/\text  {annual demand}$. The parameter-free physics curve $b=\lambda /(1+\lambda )$ (navy) tracks the measured burden of the 135 synthetic networks (grey) and Memmingen (star) with $R^2=\textbf  {0.87}$. Shaded bands give the screening decision (copperplate/adder, calibrate an adder, or resolve nodes) read off from $\lambda $ alone, without solving.}}{25}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rule}{{6}{25}{Fidelity design rule. The cost gap a copperplate misses (the loss burden) against the a-priori loss number $\lambda =\text {annual loss}/\text {annual demand}$. The parameter-free physics curve $b=\lambda /(1+\lambda )$ (navy) tracks the measured burden of the 135 synthetic networks (grey) and Memmingen (star) with $R^2=\textbf {0.87}$. Shaded bands give the screening decision (copperplate/adder, calibrate an adder, or resolve nodes) read off from $\lambda $ alone, without solving}{figure.6}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Drift of a frozen loss adder against trunk pipe length across the 135 synthetic networks. An adder calibrated on any one network mis-estimates the loss burden on others by a mean of 23.5 and up to 40.1 percentage points of cost, which is why the node-resolved model, computing the loss endogenously, is the transferable one.}}{26}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:drift}{{7}{26}{Drift of a frozen loss adder against trunk pipe length across the 135 synthetic networks. An adder calibrated on any one network mis-estimates the loss burden on others by a mean of 23.5 and up to 40.1 percentage points of cost, which is why the node-resolved model, computing the loss endogenously, is the transferable one}{figure.7}{}}
